From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 02:45:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ACPI: processor_idle: let suspend-to-idle reach the NVIDIA
 N1x DRIPS state

The N1x's _LPI reports its platform states, the deepest being
Standby-DRIPS, with a minimum residency of 0xFFFFFFFF and the cluster or
package state's exit latency (DRIPS: 966 us against the package state's
967 us) until \_SB.PEPD.ELST(1) sets MPEL. Suspend-to-idle picks the
deepest state by strictly increasing exit latency, so it stops at the
package state and the SoC never reaches DRIPS.

Calling ELST(1) the way Windows' platform extension does is not an option:
Windows only enters DRIPS once every device constraint is met, while
Linux's idle governors pick it as soon as its 1.4 ms residency fits, with
devices running, and the system hangs at boot.

Keep the residency, so the governors never choose these states, and add
DRIPS's wake latency (0x19D2 us) to their exit latency so that
suspend-to-idle reaches DRIPS.
---
 drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
@@ -910,16 +910,25 @@
 	return -EINVAL;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The NVIDIA N1x's DRIPS wake latency, from its _LPI with \_SB.PEPD.MPEL set
+ * (Standby-DRIPS: 0x19D2 us).
+ */
+#define ACPI_N1X_DRIPS_WAKE_LATENCY_US	6610
+
 static void acpi_processor_setup_lpi_states(struct acpi_processor *pr)
 {
 	int i;
 	struct acpi_lpi_state *lpi;
 	struct cpuidle_state *state;
 	struct cpuidle_driver *drv = &acpi_idle_driver;
+	bool n1x;
 
 	if (!pr->flags.has_lpi)
 		return;
 
+	n1x = acpi_dev_present("NVDA6210", NULL, -1);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < pr->power.count && i < CPUIDLE_STATE_MAX; i++) {
 		lpi = &pr->power.lpi_states[i];
 
@@ -928,6 +937,20 @@
 		strscpy(state->desc, lpi->desc, CPUIDLE_DESC_LEN);
 		state->exit_latency = lpi->wake_latency;
 		state->target_residency = lpi->min_residency;
+		/*
+		 * The N1x reports its platform states, the deepest of them being
+		 * DRIPS, with a minimum residency of 0xFFFFFFFF and the cluster or
+		 * package state's exit latency, unless \_SB.PEPD.ELST(1) has set
+		 * MPEL. Windows' platform extension calls ELST(1), but only enters
+		 * DRIPS once every device constraint is met; with the real values
+		 * Linux's idle governors pick DRIPS while devices are running and
+		 * the system hangs at boot. Keep the residency, so the governors
+		 * never choose these states, and add DRIPS's wake latency, so that
+		 * suspend-to-idle, which takes the deepest state by exit latency,
+		 * reaches DRIPS instead of stopping at the package state.
+		 */
+		if (n1x && lpi->min_residency == U32_MAX)
+			state->exit_latency += ACPI_N1X_DRIPS_WAKE_LATENCY_US;
 		state->flags |= arch_get_idle_state_flags(lpi->arch_flags);
 		state->enter = acpi_idle_lpi_enter;
 		if (i != 0 && lpi->entry_method == ACPI_CSTATE_FFH) {
